{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Seventeen.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.966,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Talking Parrot.",
   "start": 2.566,
   "end": 3.718,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Pascoe the window cleaner has been cleaning the windows of Tidewhistle Cove for twenty years, and he has said the same thing to every customer on every single visit. Agnes Bell heard it again that very morning, as he climbed down his ladder outside the Kettle and Gull.",
   "start": 5.118,
   "end": 21.459,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Mind how you go, my lovely,\" he said, tipping his cap. \"Mind how you go.\"",
   "start": 22.309,
   "end": 27.088,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Nobody else in Tidewhistle Cove ever says it. People tease Mr Pascoe about it, and he just laughs and says it again.",
   "start": 27.938,
   "end": 35.469,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Wenna Polglaze runs the little antique shop on the corner, and she shares it with an elderly grey parrot called Admiral. Admiral is a very clever bird. He picks up any phrase he hears said a few times in a row, and then repeats it for days. At the moment, he knows how to say Good morning, Pieces of eight, and Wenna's own favourite saying, Put the kettle on.",
   "start": 36.319,
   "end": 58.356,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Thursday afternoon Wenna popped out for half an hour, leaving the shop locked and Admiral on his perch. Three people had keys to the shop, because each of them sometimes looked after it for her: Tamsin Trevelyan, Mr Fenwick, and Mr Pascoe, who cleaned the inside windows once a month.",
   "start": 59.206,
   "end": 77.467,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "When Wenna came back, her antique silver thimble was gone from the counter. And Admiral was bobbing up and down on his perch, saying something he had never said before.",
   "start": 78.317,
   "end": 88.515,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Mind how you go, my lovely!\" he squawked. \"Mind how you go! Mind how you go, my lovely!\"",
   "start": 89.365,
   "end": 95.146,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Wenna stopped in the doorway with her mouth open.",
   "start": 95.996,
   "end": 98.599,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "When Agnes asked around, Tamsin said she had been in her own shop all afternoon, and Mr Fenwick said he had been at the grocer's. All three keyholders said they had not been in Wenna's shop that day.",
   "start": 99.449,
   "end": 111.651,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who took the silver thimble?",
   "start": 112.751,
   "end": 115.269,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 116.469,
   "end": 117.493,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 118.393,
   "end": 123.619,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 128.119,
   "end": 129.143,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Pascoe took the thimble.",
   "start": 130.243,
   "end": 132.078,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Admiral only picks up a phrase when he hears it said several times in a row. When Wenna left, he didn't know the phrase mind how you go, my lovely. When she came back half an hour later, he couldn't stop saying it. So somebody had been in the shop with him that half hour, saying it over and over.",
   "start": 132.928,
   "end": 150.507,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mind how you go, my lovely is the phrase Mr Pascoe has said to every customer for twenty years, and the one Agnes heard him say twice that very morning. Mr Pascoe also had a key, so the locked door was no obstacle.",
   "start": 151.357,
   "end": 165.565,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Pascoe confessed. He had dropped in to clean the windows a day early, chatted away to Admiral while he worked, and then the thimble had caught his eye. His late mother had owned one just like it. He gave it back, and Wenna, quite touched, let him buy it at a very kind price. Admiral still says mind how you go, my lovely, to every customer who walks out the door.",
   "start": 166.415,
   "end": 188.516,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
